<!-- WHY VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Why Visit Pakistan</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#128747;</div><h3>Karakoram Highway</h3><p>Drive one of the world's most spectacular roads — the legendary Karakoram Highway connecting China and Pakistan across the Khunjerab Pass (4,693m). Carved through some of earth's most dramatic mountain scenery, this engineering marvel is the highest paved international border crossing on the planet.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9968;</div><h3>Hunza Valley</h3><p>Discover Hunza, often called paradise on earth. Surrounded by 7,000-meter peaks including Rakaposhi (7,788m) and Ultar Sar, this impossibly green valley offers breathtaking landscapes, ancient forts, glacier treks, and the famously long-lived Hunza people with their legendary hospitality.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127963;</div><h3>Lahore — Mughal Splendor</h3><p>Step into the grandeur of Mughal architecture in Lahore. From the magnificent Badshahi Mosque and Lahore Fort to the exquisite Shalimar Gardens, this ancient city was a vital Silk Road trading hub and remains the cultural heart of Pakistan.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">📘</div><h3>Ancient Gandhara</h3><p>Walk the paths where Buddhism flourished and spread along the Silk Road. Taxila, one of the world's oldest universities, and the Gandhara archaeological sites preserve stunning Buddhist stupas, monasteries, and Greco-Buddhist art that fused Eastern and Western civilizations.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TOP ATTRACTIONS -->
<section class="section section-alt"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Top Attractions</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="tours-grid">
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Hunza Valley" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#1</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Hunza Valley &amp; Karimabad</h3><p>The crown jewel of northern Pakistan. Karimabad, the main town, offers spectacular views of Rakaposhi, Ultar Sar, and Ladyfinger Peak. Explore the 700-year-old Baltit Fort perched above the valley, wander through terraced apricot orchards, and experience the legendary warmth of Hunza hospitality. Don't miss sunrise over the valley — it's pure magic.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Lahore Fort" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#2</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Lahore Fort &amp; Badshahi Mosque</h3><p>Two UNESCO World Heritage masterpieces standing side by side in the heart of Lahore. The Lahore Fort (Shahi Qila) is a sprawling Mughal palace complex with the stunning Sheesh Mahal (Palace of Mirrors). Next door, the Badshahi Mosque — one of the world's largest — can hold 100,000 worshippers under its three magnificent marble domes and eight minarets.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/central-asia-architecture.jpg" alt="Fairy Meadows" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#3</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Fairy Meadows &amp; Nanga Parbat</h3><p>One of the most stunning viewpoints on earth. After a thrilling jeep ride on one of the world's most dangerous roads, a short trek leads to Fairy Meadows — an alpine plateau at 3,300m facing the mighty Nanga Parbat (8,126m), the ninth-highest mountain in the world and the western anchor of the Himalayas. The sunrise view of the "Killer Mountain" is unforgettable.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Taxila" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#4</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Taxila — Ancient Buddhist University</h3><p>A UNESCO World Heritage site and one of the most important archaeological treasures of the Silk Road. Taxila was a major center of Buddhist learning from the 5th century BCE to the 2nd century CE. Explore the well-preserved ruins of monasteries, stupas, and the ancient university where scholars from across Asia came to study philosophy, medicine, and art.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Islamabad" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#5</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Islamabad — The Green Capital</h3><p>Pakistan's modern, meticulously planned capital nestled against the Margalla Hills. Visit the iconic Faisal Mosque — the largest in South Asia with its striking contemporary design — hike the Margalla Hills trails for panoramic city views, explore the Lok Virsa Museum for Pakistan's rich cultural heritage, and enjoy the serene Saidpur Village with its craft shops and restaurants.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- BEST TIME TO VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127800;</div><h3>Spring (Mar-May)</h3><p>The ideal season for most of Pakistan. Northern valleys burst into bloom with cherry and apricot blossoms, temperatures are mild (15-25&deg;C in Islamabad, cooler in the mountains), and skies are generally clear. The Karakoram Highway typically reopens in April. Perfect for trekking, photography, and cultural exploration.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127774;</div><h3>Autumn (Sep-Nov)</h3><p>The golden season — arguably even better than spring. Clear skies, comfortable temperatures, and spectacular autumn colors paint the Hunza Valley in shades of gold and crimson. The harvest season brings fresh apricots, walnuts, and apples. October-November offers the most reliable weather for mountain views and high-altitude trekking.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9924;</div><h3>Summer (Jun-Aug)</h3><p>Best for high mountain adventures and the Karakoram Highway. While the southern plains (Lahore, Islamabad) can be hot (35-45&deg;C), the northern mountains are in their prime with lush green valleys, open passes, and ideal trekking conditions. This is the best window for Fairy Meadows, Hunza trekking, and crossing the Khunjerab Pass into China.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#10052;</div><h3>Winter (Dec-Feb)</h3><p>Cold in the north with snow closing the Karakoram Highway and most trekking routes. Temperatures can drop to -20&deg;C in the mountains. However, Lahore and the southern cities are pleasant (5-15&deg;C) — perfect for exploring Mughal architecture, museums, and enjoying hearty Pakistani cuisine with far fewer tourists around.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

